+   - **After the gate passes, amend the message with the gate record - and CHAIN THE STEPS so a refusal
+     stops the script** (one block, no extra step in the procedure):
+     ```sh
+     msg=$(mktemp)
+     if bash ~/fleet/bin/fleet-land-message --gate <merged-sha> <bead> fleet/<x> > "$msg"; then
+         git commit --amend -F "$msg" && git push origin HEAD:<default>
+     else
+         echo "gate record refused: NOT amending and NOT pushing" >&2
+     fi
+     ```
+     **Do NOT use `-F <(...)` process substitution here.** When the helper refuses, the substitution
+     yields an EMPTY message; `git commit --amend -F` then either aborts with "empty commit message"
+     or, on a path where the amend already has a message, silently leaves a body with no gate record -
+     which is the failure this block exists to prevent (agents-qngy). That is how the commit body gets the gate result instead of a claim that a gate ran. **The helper
      records the TREE as well as the commit sha**, because `fleet-check` caches by tree and the amend itself
      changes the sha: the tree is the object the gate actually measured, and it is what a later reader can
      re-check with `fleet-check --status <sha>`.
